---

<div class:list={["fx-page", embedded && "fx-page--embedded"]} data-features-content>
    <section class="fx-hero scroll-fade-in">
      <p class="fx-kicker">The Business OS</p>
      <h1 class="fx-h1">
        Runs the whole business — not a pile of separate apps.
      </h1>
      <p class="fx-lead">
        Contacts, billing, projects, inbox, scheduling, newsletters, social metrics,
        Siri, and white-label branding — one login, one AI agent, and a client portal
        that's already open on their phone. Add industry plugins for the business
        you're actually in.
      </p>
      <div class="fx-cta-row">
        <a class="fx-btn fx-btn--primary" href="/demo">
          Demo now
          <ArrowRight size={16} strokeWidth={2} aria-hidden="true" />
        </a>
        <a class="fx-btn fx-btn--ghost" href={quoteHref}>Get a quote</a>
      </div>
      <div class="fx-chips">
        <span class="fx-chip">Always-on AI agent</span>
        <span class="fx-chip">Siri-ready</span>
        <span class="fx-chip">White labeled</span>
        <span class="fx-chip">Read receipts & engagement</span>
        <span class="fx-chip">Plug in only what you need</span>
        <a class="fx-chip fx-chip--link" href="#plan">Installation tiers &amp; pricing</a>
      </div>
    </section>

    {
      showLogoStack && (
        <section class="fx-stack scroll-fade-in" aria-label="Apps this platform replaces">
          <p class="fx-stack-kicker">Replace the stack</p>
          <h2 class="fx-stack-h2">
            One platform instead of a dozen monthly subscriptions.
          </h2>
          <p class="fx-stack-lead">
            Most small businesses pay separately for email, CRM, invoicing, scheduling,
            AI, file storage, e-sign, and project tools. {company.name} is built to
            absorb that whole layer — so your clients stop juggling logins and line items.
          </p>
          <BrandLogoGrid folder="replaced-apps" />
          <p class="fx-stack-note">
            Keep Gmail or Outlook for personal mail if you want — the OS handles CRM,
            billing, projects, scheduling, AI, and the client portal that used to
            require all of the above.
          </p>
        </section>
      )
    }

    <section
      class="fx-pricing scroll-fade-in"
      aria-label="Installation plan and pricing"
      id="plan"
    >
      <span id="pricing" hidden aria-hidden="true"></span>
      <p class="fx-pricing-kicker">Installation plan</p>
      <h2 class="fx-pricing-h2">Four tiers — mapped to the features below.</h2>
      <p class="fx-pricing-lead">
        Every install starts at full tier price for month one. Each month after
        that, the fee drops by half until month four — then it holds steady for
        as long as you run the platform. Higher tiers include everything in the
        tiers below, plus the modules listed for that tier.
      </p>

      <div class="fx-pricing-table-wrap">
        <table class="fx-pricing-table">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Tier</th>
              <th scope="col">Plan</th>
              <th scope="col">Month 1</th>
              <th scope="col">Month 2</th>
              <th scope="col">Month 3</th>
              <th scope="col">Month 4+</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {
              [...INSTALLATION_TIERS].reverse().map((t) => (
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row">Tier {t.tier}</th>
                  <td class="fx-pricing-plan-name">{t.name}</td>
                  <td>{formatInstallUsd(t.month1)}</td>
                  <td>{formatInstallUsd(t.month2)}</td>
                  <td>{formatInstallUsd(t.month3)}</td>
                  <td>{formatInstallUsd(t.month4Plus)}/mo</td>
                </tr>
              ))
            }
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <div class="fx-tier-grid">
        {
          [...INSTALLATION_TIERS].reverse().map((t) => (
            <article class="fx-tier-card">
              <header class="fx-tier-card-head">
                <p class="fx-tier-card-kicker">Tier {t.tier}</p>
                <h3 class="fx-tier-card-title">{t.name}</h3>
                <p class="fx-tier-card-summary">{t.summary}</p>
                <p class="fx-tier-card-price">
                  {formatInstallUsd(t.month1)} → {formatInstallUsd(t.month4Plus)}/mo
                </p>
              </header>
              <ul class="fx-tier-feature-list">
                {
                  t.features.map((f) => (
                    <li>
                      {
                        f.href ? (
                          <a href={f.href}>{f.label}</a>
                        ) : (
                          f.label
                        )
                      }
                    </li>
                  ))
                }
              </ul>
            </article>
          ))
        }
      </div>

      <p class="fx-pricing-note">
        Example: Tier 1 (Full platform) is {formatInstallUsd(5000)} the first month,
        {formatInstallUsd(2500)} the second, {formatInstallUsd(1250)} the third, then{" "}
        {formatInstallUsd(625)} every month after that. Tier 4 (Core OS) starts at{" "}
        {formatInstallUsd(2000)} — same 50% step-down each month. Industry plugins
        and agency tools are Tier 1; most service businesses land on Tier 2 or 3.
      </p>

      <div class="fx-cta-row">
        <a class="fx-btn fx-btn--ghost" href={quoteHref}>Get a quote for your tier</a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="fx-list scroll-fade-in" aria-label="Core features">
      <h2 class="fx-list-h2">Baseline baked in</h2>
